<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dmitri Mendeleev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Dmitri Ivanovich Mendeleev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dmitri Mendeleev was a Russian chemist known for formulating the periodic law and creating a version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periodic-table">periodic table</a> of elements, which he presented to the Russian Chemical Society in 1869. Born in Verkhnie Aremzyani near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobolsk">Tobolsk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, he used the periodic law to correct the accepted properties of some known elements, such as the valence and atomic weight of uranium, and to predict the properties of three elements not yet discovered: germanium, gallium and scandium. He was one of the founders of the Russian Chemical Society in 1868 and also worked on protectionist trade and agriculture. The synthetic element mendelevium is named in his honour.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dmitri-mendeleev.jpg" alt="Dmitri Mendeleev" width="330" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Upload: Germansociety2014 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitri_Mendeleev.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mendeleev was born on 8 February 1834 (27 January in the old style) to Ivan Pavlovich Mendeleev, a school principal, and Maria Dmitrievna Kornilieva, from a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobolsk">Tobolsk</a> merchants. He was the youngest of 17 children, of whom 14 lived to be baptised, according to his brother Pavel. His father went blind and lost his teaching post, and his mother restarted the family's glass factory. When Mendeleev was 13 his father died and a fire destroyed the factory.</p><p>In 1849 his mother took him across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where the university would not accept him, and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, where he entered the Main Pedagogical Institute in 1850. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> between 1859 and 1861 and won the Demidov Prize for his textbook Organic Chemistry. He became a professor at Saint Petersburg in 1865.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>While writing his textbook Principles of Chemistry, published in two volumes between 1868 and 1870, Mendeleev noticed patterns among the elements, and he said later that he saw the whole arrangement in a dream. In 1863 there were 56 known elements, and John Newlands and Lothar Meyer had proposed tables of their own, unknown to him.</p><p>He called the three elements he predicted ekasilicon, ekaaluminium and ekaboron, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> prefixes; they proved to be germanium, gallium and scandium. Gallium was found in 1875 and germanium in 1886. He was wrong in assuming that the accepted atomic weight of tellurium was at fault. He also studied the origins and uses of petroleum, invented the smokeless powder pyrocollodion, and from 1892 directed the Central Bureau of Weights and Measures, introducing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metric-system">metric system</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His divorce and remarriage in 1882 contributed to his failure to be admitted to the Russian Academy of Sciences. In 1906 the Nobel Committee for Chemistry recommended him for the prize, but at the full meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-academy">Swedish Academy</a> a member proposed Henri Moissan, and Svante Arrhenius pressed for rejection, arguing that the periodic system was too old. The academy chose Moissan by one vote.</p><p>Mendeleev died of influenza in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1907 aged 72 and was buried at Literatorskie Mostki cemetery. A widely repeated story that he set the 40 per cent strength of vodka is false: the government fixed it in 1843, when he was nine. A lunar crater and a mineral, mendeleevite-Ce, bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dmitri Mendeleev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
